            <ArticleTitle>Treatment of distal occlusion of Krupin eye valve with disk using cannular flush.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>102-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 64-year-old man with neovascular glaucoma underwent implantation of a Krupin valve with disk. On the fifth postoperative day, the patient reported pain in the operative eye; examination revealed a hyphema, an intraocular pressure (IOP) of 52 mm Hg, and no visible bleb. A bleb was reestablished and the IOP markedly decreased following irrigation of the shunt using a 30-gauge cannula through a paracentesis site.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
